topics.studyContent:
ES6 (ECMAScript 2015) and later versions introduced major syntax improvements. Arrow functions (=>) provide a shorter syntax and do not have their own this binding, making them ideal for callbacks and methods that reference the outer context. Destructuring lets you unpack values from arrays or objects: const { name, age } = user; or const [first, ...rest] = arr;

The spread operator (...) expands an iterable into individual elements: [...arr1, ...arr2] merges arrays, {...obj1, ...obj2} merges objects. The rest parameter collects remaining arguments: function(a, b, ...rest). Template literals use backticks and ${expression} for string interpolation, eliminating messy string concatenation.

ES6 modules use export and import instead of CommonJS require(). Named exports: export const foo = ...; import { foo } from './file'. Default exports: export default MyComponent; import MyComponent from './file'. Optional chaining (?.) safely accesses nested properties: user?.address?.city — returns undefined instead of throwing. Nullish coalescing (??) returns the right-hand value only when the left is null or undefined.
